{"name":"Deep Viridian","hex":"#4b6443","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"deep","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Extending, reaching or positioned far from a point of reference, especially downwards.\nExtending far down from the top, or surface, to the bottom, literally or figuratively.","Extending far down from the top, or surface, to the bottom, literally or figuratively."]},{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["Far, especially far down through something or into something, physically or figuratively.","In a profound, not superficial, manner."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/deep"},{"term":"viridian","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A bluish-green pigment made from chromium sesquioxide.","A bluish-green color."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a bluish green colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/viridian"}],"entries":[{"title":"Viridian","blocks":[{"text":"Viridian is a blue-green pigment, a hydrated chromium(III) oxide, of medium saturation and relatively dark in value. It is composed of a majority of green, followed by blue. The first recorded use of viridian as a color name in English was in the 1860s. Viridian takes its name from the Latin viridis, meaning \"green\". The pigment was first prepared in mid-19th-century Paris and remains available from several US manufacturers as prepared artists' colors in all media."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/Chromium%28III%29-oxide-sample.jpg/330px-Chromium%28III%29-oxide-sample.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Viridian"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Viridian%28Pigment_Green_18%29_%28Left%29_And_Phthalocyanine_Green%28Pigment_Green_7%29_%28Right%29_Gouache.jpg/500px-Viridian%28Pigment_Green_18%29_%28Left%29_And_Phthalocyanine_Green%28Pigment_Green_7%29_%28Right%29_Gou